      "learned": "A salt is an ionic compound formed when the H⁺ of an acid is replaced by a metal ion (or ammonium ion). Salts can be prepared by: (1) neutralisation (acid + base); (2) direct combination of a metal with a non-metal (e.g., Na + Cl₂ → NaCl); (3) reaction of a metal with an acid (e.g., Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂); (4) reaction of a metal oxide/carbonate with an acid. Salt solutions are not always neutral — they can be acidic (e.g., NH₄Cl) or alkaline (e.g., Na₂CO₃) due to hydrolysis. The Lake Magadi trona (Na₂CO₃·NaHCO₃·2H₂O) is a naturally occurring salt deposit of enormous economic value to Kenya. Pedagogical note: The variety of salts from everyday Kenyan contexts (fertiliser, cement, plaster, supplements) powerfully corrects the misconception that 'salt = NaCl only.'",
